  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can BSF really be raised indoors year-round in a cold climate?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes — peer-reviewed protocols (Diener et al.; Oonincx et al. 2018)
        document successful low-cost, year-round laboratory rearing, and
        hobbyists are doing it in 5b basements with off-the-shelf gear. The
        operating constraint is the cage microclimate (25–32&nbsp;°C, 50–60% RH,
        12&nbsp;h of bright full-spectrum light), not your outdoor weather.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Do I need a UV reptile bulb, or will a regular halogen work?</h3>
      <p>
        A quartz halogen worklight with the protective glass removed passes
        enough UVA to trigger BSF mating — Tomberlin &amp; Sheppard (2010)
        measured ~61% of sunlight efficacy. Reptile UVA/UVB bulbs work too,
        and BSF-tuned LEDs (Hoc 2020) actually outperformed halogen in
        controlled tests. The "best" choice depends on cage volume, distance,
        and how much heat you can dump in your room.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How big does the love cage need to be?</h3>
      <p>
        The often-cited benchmark is a 1.5&nbsp;m vertical flight column for
        male display behavior, but practical hobbyist setups run successfully
        in 60–120&nbsp;cm cages. Bigger is better up to a point; a 48&nbsp;in
        butterfly tent is a workable starter size for ≤200 adults.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Are recommendations on this site unbiased?</h3>
      <p>
        We earn an Amazon affiliate commission when you buy through our links,
        so we have a financial interest in <em>some</em> link being clicked.
        We pick gear based on what we actually use; if a product isn't worth
        buying, we just don't write about it. <a href="/disclosure/">Full disclosure here.</a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I send you photos of my setup for feedback?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes — that's the most useful thing you can do. Photos of your cage,
        bin, light positioning, and a hygrometer reading help us actually
        diagnose problems instead of guessing. Send them to the email above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

